@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: why sbx's own policy decides egress, why the egress row shows its baseline read-only, and what the Kit may add
' covers: internal/sbx/sbx.go
' covers: internal/run/choices.go
' covers: internal/sbx/baseline_test.go
' covers: internal/sbx/policy.go
' covers: internal/sbx/baseline_live_test.go
' covers: internal/run/topology.go
' covers: internal/run/params.go (seedFromCache)
' covers: internal/run/params_test.go
' covers: internal/run/run_test.go (TestEgressRowShowsTheHostBaseline, network.allow)
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (Spec: the Kit's network.allow; WarnBaseline, ReachHosts)
' covers: internal/backend/sandbox/domain_form_test.go (TestTheKitWritesNoProviderOrHarnessHosts)
' covers: internal/posture/posture.go (EnforcedBy, Observability)
' covers: e2e/sbx_egress_test.go
'   the backend that refuses without sbx . _spec/internal/sbx/sandbox-backend.puml
'   who holds the key on this backend .... _spec/internal/sbx/credential-path.puml
'   the domain form the Kit writes ....... _spec/internal/sbx/kit-domain-form.puml
'
' ── DECIDED 2026-10-16: THE TIER VOCABULARY IS RETIRED ─────────────────────
' The Executor, on mapping open/allowlist/review onto sbx: "Nothing, `sbx`
' handles this. Drop that param". And on review: "Retire review, map the rest".
' MEASURED in the working tree:
'   * `--egress-mode` survives only as a HIDDEN flag that refuses the run:
'     "--egress-mode is retired: sbx's own network policy decides egress
'     (`sbx policy ls`, `sbx policy allow network <host>`); remove the flag"
'     (cmd/proveo/main.go, pinned in main_test.go). egress.Modes, Canonical,
'     ValidMode, entrypoint.CanonicalTiers and the egress_vocabulary contract
'     test are deleted, and the retired aliases broker / firewall / proxy with
'     them.
'   * proveo writes NO tier-driven entry into the Kit's permissions.network.
'     allow: no harness `capabilities.hosts`, no provider reach
'     (credentials.ReachableHosts is deleted), no custom domains, no
'     PROVEO_EGRESS_PROVIDER_DOMAINS. sandbox.Spec adds only what an add-on wires
'     — host Ollama localhost:11434, the host CDP port, the host adb port and the
'     adb install hosts — plus an own-agent Kit's credential-injection domains.
'     A claudecode mixin Kit therefore carries an EMPTY allow list
'     (internal/run/run_test.go: "the Kit names no hosts";
'     TestTheKitWritesNoProviderOrHarnessHosts in
'     internal/backend/sandbox/domain_form_test.go).
'   * sbx's own policy decides egress: the global baseline plus whatever the
'     operator adds with `sbx policy`. proveo reports it and never sets it.
'   * posture: the "egress tier", "brokered", "reachable hosts" and "harness
'     hosts" rows are gone. EnforcedBy() = "sbx — the host's network policy +
'     credential proxy"; Observability() names sbx's policy log, saved at
'     teardown to <egDir>/sbx/policy-log.json.
'   * the review tier and its consent gate are retired with internal/reviewgate.
'     internal/ptyproxy stays: every interactive sbx run uses it.
'   * manifest Capabilities.Egress and agentsettings Choice.Egress are deleted.
'     Fingerprint keeps an empty first slot (retiredEgressSlot) so cached keys
'     stay stable.
' [Inference] Under deny-all a mixin agent reaches its provider through sbx's
' own built-in agent definition, not through anything proveo adds. Unverified
' against sbx's agent kits in this pass.
' What proveo does instead of allowlisting, MEASURED in the working tree at this
' pass (the code was still moving): run.launchSandbox calls
' sandbox.WarnBaseline(sandbox.ReachHosts(man, detected)). On an allow-all host
' it notes "sbx's global network policy allows every host; sbx decides this
' run's egress". Otherwise it prints the operator's own command —
' `sbx policy allow network <hosts>` — built from the manifest's
' capabilities.hosts plus the capability-filtered detected providers' hosts,
' through sbx.DomainPatterns. proveo names the reach; the operator grants it.
'
' ── REACH THE BASELINE DOES NOT GOVERN ────────────────────────────────────
' The baseline and the Kit allow govern traffic that leaves the sandbox. An
' add-on that hands the agent a HOST service also hands it whatever that
' service can reach from the host, outside every sbx rule. Each one is an
' explicit per-run opt-in that warns at launch:
'   host Chrome (CDP)   the browser fetches from the host network
'                       (host-browser-cdp.puml)
'   android (host adb)  host:connect, reverse and the emulator's NIC dial from
'                       the host; accepted 2026-09-29, relay deferred
'                       (host-android-adb.puml, ACCEPTED RISK)
'
' ── HISTORY: THE TIER VOCABULARY, 2026-09-16 TO 2026-10-16 ─────────────────
' When the docker+egress diagrams were retired (2026-09-16) the words open /
' allowlist / review moved here as a CLI surface and a declared intent, pinned by
' internal/contract/egress_vocabulary_test.go (`egress.Modes()` ==
' `entrypoint.CanonicalTiers`, the aliases broker / firewall / proxy rejected past
' the CLI). The tier was inert against the Kit allowlist the whole time:
' sandbox.go wrote the allowlist from harness hosts plus
' credentials.ReachableHosts and never read the tier, while run.go reported
' `EgressTier: p.Mode`. So an sbx run PRINTED `egress tier: open` while WRITING an
' allowlist the tier could not touch. The 2026-10-16 retirement ends that split
' by deleting the tier, not by mapping it.
'
' A case that was misread as that split, kept so it is not reached again: an
' operator on an allow-all baseline saw `api.x.ai` "blocked". It was never a
' reach denial — on allow-all a Kit cannot narrow. The Kit carried the Squid
' `dstdomain` form `.x.ai`, which sbx does not enforce, so the credential was
' never injected and the request left unauthenticated
' (_spec/internal/sbx/kit-domain-form.puml).
'
' ── THE ROW WAS DEAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' On the sbx backend the egress axis offered open | allowlist | review and none
' of the three did anything: open and allowlist produced an identical sandbox,
' and review fell back to docker. A three-option risk axis on which nothing
' moves is worse than no axis: it tells the operator they chose a posture. The
' row became sbx's baseline, read-only; the tiers are now deleted outright.
'
' ── WHAT ACTUALLY GOVERNS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
' sbx's GLOBAL network baseline — allow-all | balanced | deny-all. It is the only
' lever, and that is a property of how sbx composes policy, not a proveo choice:
'   * a Kit ADDS allow rules on top of the baseline; it cannot subtract.
'   * a per-sandbox deny cannot express "only the allowlist", because deny beats
'     allow — `sbx policy deny network --sandbox X "**"` blocks the allowlist too.
' So on an allow-all host the Kit allowlist ADDS REACH rather than limiting it,
' and proveo's own line (sandbox.WarnBaseline) says sbx decides this run's
' egress. The row says it in the place the operator is actually looking. Since
' 2026-10-16 the Kit adds only add-on ports and own-agent credential domains,
' so on a balanced or deny-all host those are the only reach proveo contributes;
' the rest is the `sbx policy allow network` hint described above.
'
' ── DISPLAY ONLY, AND THAT IS THE POINT ────────────────────────────────────
' The row is LOCKED. proveo reports the baseline and never sets it:
'   * it is host-wide, shared by every sandbox including ones proveo never
'     started, so a per-run prompt would rewrite other people's runs;
'   * changing it needs `sbx policy reset`, which clears EVERY policy on the host;
'   * and offering it as a per-run choice would teach the wrong intuition — that
'     this is a property of the container in front of you. It is not.
' The change hint is one sample, identical for every baseline. It carries the
' reset because `sbx policy init` alone is REJECTED once a host is initialized —
' measured: "global network policy is already initialized; use sbx policy reset
' first to change it". A hint without the reset would print a command that fails.
'
' ── READING IT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' From sbx, not by probing. `sbx policy check` can only separate allow-all from
' "something stricter", because balanced and deny-all both deny an unallowlisted
' host. `sbx policy inspect local-policy` prints the rules, and the network rows
' classify structurally:
'   allow **          -> allow-all
'   no network allow  -> deny-all
'   specific allows   -> balanced
' Unreadable stays UNREADABLE. Naming a baseline proveo could not read would put a
' boundary in front of the operator that may not exist — the same failure the dead
' row committed, in the other direction.
'
' MEASURED HOW FAR: only allow-all was observable on the development host, because
' changing the baseline needs the destructive reset. The other two fixtures are
' built from the same table shape; internal/sbx TestPolicyBaselineLive (tag
' livesbx) is what catches sbx changing that table.
'
' ── HISTORY: THE ROW'S REPORT LEAKED INTO A MODE (2026-09-15) ──────────────
' The form wrote the egress row's value — a BASELINE — into `p.Mode`, and the
' first caller to hand "allow-all" to `egress.BuildPlan` killed the run
' (`egress: unknown mode "allow-all"`). Two guards closed it: promptChoices
' stopped writing the egress selection, and seedFromCache seeded `p.Mode` only
' from a valid mode. MEASURED 2026-10-16: `p.Mode`, BuildPlan and ValidMode are
' all deleted, promptChoices reads no egress selection, and seedFromCache seeds
' no egress value. The leak has no target left. The rule it taught stays: a
' report is never written back as a choice.
'
' RESOLVED 2026-10-16: posture.EnforcedBy used to say "sbx — Kit network
' allowlist + credential proxy" whatever the baseline was, which overstated on
' an allow-all host. It now says "sbx — the host's network policy + credential
' proxy". Whether an sbx sandbox's own docker daemon can leave sbx's policy is
' still asserted only by e2e/sbx_egress_test.go's DAEMON probe, which skips on
' an allow-all host.
title The sbx egress row — sbx's policy decides; proveo shows it and adds only add-on reach

participant "operator" as OP <<app>>
participant "proveo\negressRow" as ROW <<app>>
participant "sbx policy\ninspect local-policy" as SBX <<cloud>>
participant "the Kit\nallowlist" as KIT <<data>>

OP ARROW_MAIN ROW : run <harness> (sbx backend)
ROW ARROW_MAIN SBX : read the global baseline
SBX ARROW_MAIN ROW : allow ** -> allow-all
ROW ARROW_ERROR OP : locked row, current value shown,\none runnable command to change it
KIT ARROW_OPTIONAL SBX : adds allow rules ON TOP\n(cannot subtract)

note over ROW
  **The tiers are gone (2026-10-16).** "Nothing,
  `sbx` handles this. Drop that param": the
  tiers are deleted, not mapped, and a hidden
  ""--egress-mode"" now refuses the run.
end note

note over ROW
  **A report is never written back as a choice.**
  Storing this row in ""p.Mode"" once killed a
  run. ""p.Mode"" is deleted now, so the row has
  no field to leak into.
end note

note over KIT
  **The Kit adds add-on reach only.** Host
  Ollama, CDP and adb ports, adb install hosts,
  and an own-agent Kit's credential domains. No
  harness hosts, no provider reach.
end note

note over SBX
  **Deny beats allow.** That one rule is why the
  baseline is the only lever: no per-sandbox rule
  means "confine to the allowlist", because a deny
  broad enough to confine also blocks the
  allowlist it was meant to leave open.
end note
@enduml
